{
  "reasoning": "Update video-processor/SKILL.md to enforce a single interval convention and reflect corrected timestamp alignment: opening boundary from silence-detector must be treated as an exclusive end ([start,end)) and long-pause detection must begin at that boundary. Clarify pause threshold semantics (threshold_ratio) and require normalization of all removal segments via merge/de-overlap, clamping to media duration, before ffmpeg trimming/concat and before report validation.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "op": "insert_after",
      "target": "## Mandatory end-to-end completion gate",
      "content": "\n### Time/interval conventions (MUST follow)\n\n- Use a consistent **half-open interval** convention for all segments: **`[start, end)`** in seconds.\n  - `start` is inclusive, `end` is exclusive.\n  - Require `start < end`.\n  - Duration is computed as `end - start`.\n- Treat timestamps returned by detectors as **timeline seconds** aligned to the original media. Any segment list passed downstream must follow `[start,end)`.\n- All segment operations (sorting, merging, de-overlap, inversion to keep-segments, and ffmpeg trim/atrim) must use the same `[start,end)` convention to avoid 1-frame/1-sample overlaps or gaps.\n"
    },
    {
      "op": "replace",
      "target": "3. Detect the non-teaching opening with the silence detector's result, then start long-pause detection at that returned boundary. \n4. Combine, sort, clamp, and de-overlap all removal segments.",
      "content": "3. Detect the non-teaching opening using the silence detector and interpret its returned boundary as the **corrected opening end** (the exclusive `end` of `[0, opening_end)`).\n4. Start long-pause detection **at `t = opening_end`** (do not search for pauses before the corrected opening boundary).\n5. Combine all removal segments (opening + pauses), then **normalize** them: sort, merge, de-overlap, and **clamp to `[0, media_duration)`** before any ffmpeg processing."
    },
    {
      "op": "replace",
      "target": "- every removed segment has numeric `start < end` and `duration ≈ end - start`, and the segments are sorted and non-overlapping;",
      "content": "- every removed segment uses the `[start,end)` convention with numeric `start < end`, `duration ≈ end - start`, and the final list is **sorted, merged/de-overlapped, and clamped to `[0, original_duration)`** (no overlaps, no out-of-range endpoints);"
    },
    {
      "op": "insert_after",
      "target": "## How It Works",
      "content": "\n### Segment normalization requirement (before ffmpeg)\n\nWhen multiple sources produce removal segments (e.g., opening detector + pause detector), you MUST normalize them **before** computing keep-segments and before invoking ffmpeg:\n\n1. Concatenate all removal segments.\n2. Sort by `start`.\n3. **Clamp** each segment to `[0, media_duration)`.\n4. **Merge/de-overlap** using `[start,end)` semantics:\n   - If `next.start <= current.end`, merge into `[current.start, max(current.end, next.end))`.\n   - Drop any zero/negative-length segments after clamping.\n\nThis prevents duplicated removals, negative keep intervals, and concat failures.\n"
    },
    {
      "op": "insert_after",
      "target": "## Notes",
      "content": "\n- **Pause threshold tuning:** pause detection should be driven by relative energy. `threshold_ratio` is interpreted as a ratio of the baseline/typical energy level; **smaller values remove more (more aggressive), larger values remove less (more conservative)**. When tuning, keep opening removal fixed first, then adjust pause `min_duration` (e.g., ~2s) and `threshold_ratio` to remove true long pauses while preserving teaching.\n"
    }
  ]
}

